{
  "abstract": "We give fully explicit mollified second-moment bounds on the critical line and\ndeduce exponential near-critical concentration in two short-interval regimes.\nOn every interval $(T,T+T^{2/5}]$, for $T\\ge e^{1000}$ and\n$50\\le c<(\\log T)/2$,\nthe fraction of nontrivial zeta zeros, counted with multiplicity, satisfying\n$|\\Re\\rho-1/2|\\ge c/\\log T$ is less than\n$0.01e^{-0.075(c-50)}$. In particular, more than99% are within\n$50/\\log T$ of the critical line. Retaining the complete nonlinear\nsaddle phase gives, on every $(T,T+T^{33/100}]$ with\n$T\\ge e^{1000000}$, the exceptional-fraction upper bound\n$0.01e^{-0.00099(c-3000)}$ for $3000\\le c<(\\log T)/2$.\nThus more than99% lie within $3000/\\log T$ at that shorter scale.\nThe argument\nGaussian-averages the complete Motohashi divisor identity as developed by\nIshikawa and Matsumoto, uses a polynomial weight to cancel its continuation\npole, and bounds both frequency signs and the entire divisor tail.\nThe shorter-scale estimate uses a uniform stationary approximation and\nclassical fifth-derivative differencing of the full divisor sum, including\nits modular phase. Gaussian projection retains the cancellation from subtracting the low\nDirichlet frequencies, and strip convexity gives a centered mean to the\nright of the line. An explicit Littlewood argument includes both horizontal\nboundaries and the zero-count denominator. We also give the elementary\n$4.2/c$ bound for every $c>0$ on the $T^{2/5}$ intervals.\nThe classical $27/82$ short-interval mechanism is not new;\nthe contribution is the explicit bound and starting height. The result allows\nindividual off-line zeros and does not establish the Riemann hypothesis.",
